                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} gpd(j)\llbracket T\rrbracket_{C/X}\to \llbracket T\rrbracket_{D/X}.
            For discussion about whether this works, see the nForum thread for this page.
          %p
            Note that to model polymorphic simple type theory such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/System+F"} System F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
            be
            = succeed ";" do
              %em complete
            = succeed "." do
              %em objects
            %em
              full subcategory of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            can have products indexed by its own set of objects; this (and a bit more) was shown in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Reynolds84"} Reynolds 84
            %a{:href => "#Pitts87"} Pitts 87
            via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            of a syntactic model into its topos of presheaves.
          %p
            In contrast, something more like a true complete small category may be necessary to model an impredicative dependent type theory such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/calculus+of+constructions"} calculus of constructions
            with a proof-relevant impredicative sort (traditionally called “Set”), where the impredicative universe admits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pi-types"} Pi-types
            with
            %em arbitrary
            domain.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor+theorem"} adjoint functor theorem
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Theorem
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CompleteSmallCategoriesArePosets"}
            appears as exercise D in ch.3 (p.78) in:
          %ul
            %li#Freyd64
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"}> Peter Freyd
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Abelian Categories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/3/tr3abs.html"} TAC reprints no. 3 (2003)
          %p Reviewed as Thm. 2.1 in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Shulman"}> Michael Shulman
              ,
              %em Set theory for category theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0810.1279"}> arXiv:0810.1279
              )
          %p The original reference for the complete small categories in the effective topos is:
          %ul
            %li#Hyland88
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Hyland"}> Martin Hyland
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A small complete category
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://webdpmms.maths.cam.ac.uk/~jmeh1/Research/Oldpapers/smallcomplete88.pdf"} pdf
          %p It was expanded further in the following paper, which discusses the strong/weak completeness issue and the relation to stacks in more detail:
          %ul
            %li#HRR90
              Hyland, J. M., Robinson, E. P. and Rosolini, G. (1990), The Discrete Objects in the Effective Topos. Proceedings of the London Mathematical Society, s3-60: 1-36.
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1112/plms/s3-60.1.1"} doi:10.1112/plms/s3-60.1.1
          %p
            There was much related activity around that time, by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"} Peter Freyd
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eugenio+Moggi"} Eugenio Moggi
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Pitts"} Andrew Pitts
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Reynolds"} John Reynolds
          %ul
            %li#Reynolds84
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Reynolds"}> John Reynolds
                , Polymorphism is not set-theoretic. In: Kahn G., MacQueen D.B., Plotkin G. (eds) Semantics of Data Types. SDT 1984. Lecture Notes in Computer Science, vol 173. Springer, Berlin, Heidelberg
            %li#Pitts87
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andy+Pitts"}> Andy Pitts
                , Polymorphism is Set Theoretic, Constructively. In Category Theory and Computer Science, Proceedings, Edinburgh 1987, Lecture Notes in Computer Science Vol. 283 (Springer-Verlag, Berlin, 1987), pp 12-39,
                %a{:href => "https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/~amp12/papers/polist/polist.pdf"} PDF
          %p A more recent survey of this type of model for polymorphic type theory is in
          %ul
            %li
              Andrea Asperti and Simone Martini, Categorical models of polymorphism, Information and Computation, Volume 99, Issue 1, 1992, Pages 1-79,
              = succeed "90024-A.)" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0890-5401(92"} DOI
          %p
            The idea of using a replete complete subcategory which is essentially small in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+set+theory"} IZF
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#RS04
              %p
                Using Synthetic Domain Theory to Prove Operational Properties of a Polymorphic Programming Language Based on Strictness“, Guiseppe Rosolini and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Simpson"} Alex Simpson
                %a{:href => "https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Giuseppe_Rosolini/publication/237887060_Using_Synthetic_Domain_Theory_to_Prove_Operational_Properties_of_a_Polymorphic_Programming_Language_Based_on_Strictness/links/0deec53356d020dc85000000/Using-Synthetic-Domain-Theory-to-Prove-Operational-Properties-of-a-Polymorphic-Programming-Language-Based-on-Strictness.pdf"} ResearchGate
            %li#MS09
              %p
                Relational parametricity for computational effects, Rasmus Møgelberg and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Simpson"} Alex Simpson
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0906.5488"} arxiv
          %p The interplay with colimits is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dusko+Pavlovic"}> Duško Pavlović
              ,
              %em On completeness and cocompleteness in and around small categories
              , APAL
              %strong 74
              (1995) pp.121-152.
        .revisedby
